  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The lead they built in the first five innings dissolved in one brutal collapse between the sixth and seventh.",
    "what_this_means": "Colorado came to Oracle Park and systematically dismantled a 4-2 Giants advantage. Blade Tidwell lasted only 4.1 innings, exiting with six runs already charged. The bullpen cycled through six arms trying to stanch the bleeding. Sam Hentges, JT Brubaker, Reiver Sanmartin, Keaton Winn, Trent Harris, Jason Foley. A parade of bodies that signaled panic, not control. By the ninth inning, the Rockies had scored 13 runs and the game was over as a contest. Rafael Devers kept the Giants' hopes alive with three RBI, but three runs against that barrage is a gesture, not a fight. Eleven hits produced only seven runs. The math doesn't forgive that kind of waste when the starter fails this early and the division is 23 games away.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is inconsistency in its clearest form. The offense generated baserunners and left five of them on base in RISP situations. Which is actually conversion. But it never accumulated enough volume to match the defensive catastrophe unfolding in real time. When your starter implodes before the fifth inning, the offense has to be relentless. They weren't. They were just active enough to make the final score feel less humiliating than it should have been.",
